    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-7623",
      "problem": "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x).$ Plot the graph of $y=f\\left(2x+2\\right)-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215685/assets/69e2a7112819db4536ea902c236c7d272fb8fb5ada1d114a59dcea5de212d4d5.png)",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny = f\\left(2x+2\\right) -1 = f\\left(2(x+1)\\right) - 1\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=f\\left(2(x+1)\\right) - 1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(2x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215685/assets/b9f7d5c0b8014bc3e37171efb5bcc6422573cb4dc3a3a3ed783ca10385fd9a86.png)\n\n- Then, translate it to the left by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2(x+1)\\right).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215685/assets/d4e0469b84d60010a5cd78ee9b575aec441180b615a2db5587099d65e4bf3496.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it down by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2(x+1)\\right)-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215685/assets/c6ebcaa8fd8d421de53231e381130967ec35a270b35786d17a0e4c9e98420a9e.png)",
